# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: repl-comma-command-crlf — `,help' answers a lone `"' on MS-Windows
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-repl-comma-commands

* Symptom

Reported by pjb 2026-09-27 on PF5S26BT (clautolisp 2.2.136, MS-Windows,
bash in an Emacs shell buffer):

#+begin_example
_$ ,help

"
_$
#+end_example

No comma-command worked; Lisp forms evaluated normally.

* Cause

=read-line-from-input-context= returned =READ-LINE='s result verbatim. When
the input stream does not translate CRLF (SBCL's stdin on MS-Windows, a
terminal/Emacs buffer sending CRLF), the line keeps its trailing CR. A Lisp
form does not care (CR is whitespace to the reader), but a comma line became
the command "help<CR>", unknown; the reply =? unknown command "help<CR>"=
carries the CR, and a terminal honouring it returns to column 0, so the
closing ="= overwrites the message — leaving a lone ="=.

Reproduced on macOS by piping =,help\r\n=: =? unknown command "help^M"=.

The interactor's input code had not changed since 2.0.11, so the
"regression" is environmental (what sends CRLF), not a code change.

* Resolved

clautolisp 2.2.140: =read-line-from-input-context= drops one trailing CR —
line-ending normalisation at the input boundary, as AGENTS.md asks. Every
interactor reader (REPL comma-commands, debugger / navigator command lines,
shell escape, argument prompts) goes through it. Regression test
=crlf-lines-read-like-lf-lines= (autolisp-interactor tests).
